I'll tell you that. That thing breaks down if you look at it funny.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.200
Right. And, uh, what we have here is a top-down racing game.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:20.960
And, emphasis on the top-down part, because for the most part we've gotten like isometric

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:28.460
racing games or, um, like the, the pseudo 3D ones where they, uh, you know, they, they

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.900
show the car kind of from the rear.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:37.740
Top-down, I think we've only had so far unofficially on the show as part of our Nostalgia Byte series

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.940
for Micro Machines.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.840
I don't think we've had an actual game, uh, do this approach yet for driving.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:51.980
And, I'm starting to think that maybe it was because, uh, conveying a, a sense of speed

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:58.320
top-down is a lot harder than doing it with the Rad Racer approach of just like, you know,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.360
having a city background and, and twisting turns to happen, happening very suddenly.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:09.580
Like, when you, when you're locked in at the center of the screen the entire time and, you

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.660
know, as the car turns, the camera still stays centered.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.420
Everything just kind of scrolls like, uh, in like a Super Mario Brothers kind of scale.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:27.500
I think there's a, like, it's sort of a, there's a bit of a dimensional graph that can be made

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:35.640
of like, if you're going to have like actual tracking of individual racers and have it sort

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:41.420
of exist in a time and space, um, then it can't feel fast.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.160
Because the only way to do that is to kind of like, futz it.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:51.920
Which is what I feel like all of those behind the car racers are doing.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:56.520
Like, cause you can't actually, you can't like turn around in those games.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:04.120
You can't, um, there's no actual like tracking of where your other competing vehicles are.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.380
It's just sort of like, this feels fast.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:13.520
Um, so yeah, I think that if they, that this is just how it has to be though, you know,

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.120
you can drift in this game.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:20.820
That's I'd say it's like, you know, the high level play you learn in like 20 to 30 minutes

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.080
of playing it. Yeah.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.240
And we'll get into the mechanics of the cars themselves.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:32.020
Cause I, I do agree that there is, uh, there, there's both a, uh, a skill level to the

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.220
game to get better at it.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.560
And also just like a, uh, a monetary spend, uh, as long as you have the money, you can

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:39.960
probably also win the game.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.840
So there, there's two different approaches, neither one incorrect, I suppose, if the game

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:49.240
allows you to exploit them, but just to, uh, you know, maybe pull it back a little bit.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.160
You know, this is just a racing game.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:53.700
There is no real story or characters behind them.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:59.360
It's just circuits, complete the races, get the prize money, buy the upgrades and continue

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:00.580
to do more races.

