Oh, Electrobrain.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.040
Right. You're right. Published, published by Electrobrain.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.300
Oh, okay. So there we go.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:56.360
Um, and there's a lot of things to talk about with the computer version.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.900
Uh, there's a lot to break down in terms of the similarities between the NES version and

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.600
the changes in the computer version and everything.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:07.380
But I think we could save that maybe for the end and focus on the NES version because

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:14.100
this NES version, while mostly the same, uh, you know, the, the, the core loop of the game

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:19.240
is all wrapped around that shop that, that what happens after you win money, because otherwise

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.560
you're just basically racing on different tracks.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:28.140
The, uh, the progression all happens in this, uh, screen where you're at like a car dealership,

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:33.560
where there's a, uh, parts section and a buy cars section, just like in real dealerships,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:37.400
uh, where you go get your car serviced, but it turns out you also bought your car there,

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.380
but you'll never step back in that room again, unless you want to buy another car.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.680
Like they keep themselves, uh, separated.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:44.760
It's like, oh, that's weird.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.020
I'll never see the guy who sold me my car again.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.680
Anyway, uh, the shop.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:53.680
Did you have like a traumatic experience at the, at the car dealership?

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.200
Maybe. Right.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:56.440
Maybe I liked that guy.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.440
Maybe I thought I was going to see him again in my life.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:05.900
And so between the races, you visit the garage to repair damage to the car, uh, you know,

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.000
refuel, re-air the tires, fix the engine.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.420
And then you can buy what I learned the hard way are one-time upgrades.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.180
Uh, I thought I just started this game incredibly rich and I was like, oh, I'm just going to be able to buy all these upgrades.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:24.120
Uh, and you can buy these one-time upgrades like turbo chargers and side armor.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.980
And, uh, if you can believe this guys missiles.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.900
Yeah. That's legal in this form of racing.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.820
Uh, you can buy front and rear missiles separately, of course.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:40.540
And, um, as you, uh, as you buy all those things, you use them in the following race.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:42.580
Then you compete in these races.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.080
And if you come in first, you win like $20,000.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:08:01.700
If not, you win like, I think for second and third place, but the actual loop becomes how little you can spend while still winning in order to make enough money to buy better cars, to move through the races faster.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:13.360
Because it's very easy to win $20,000 coming in first place by every kind of turbo and high speed kit and, you know, make sure your engine and body are maxed out.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:18.320
This way you can just kind of, you know, hit any wall and not worry about turning correctly and shit.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:25.420
And yeah, you'll come in first, but you will never be able to buy the new car because you'll be spending too much on the upgrade.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:33.740
So you have to think about how do I get through races without repairing my gas, my body, my tire, and my engine every single time.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.060
And that's the neat part of the game.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:47.820
Yeah, it's, it's fun. When you mentioned the, the sort of missile and body armor thing, I, I w I saw that in the manual and I was expecting some kind of post-apocalyptic Mad Max situation.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:09:01.800
And it very much is not that like when you, when you go through these, uh, these, these courses, you see like, you know, nice manicured foliage and sort of patio furniture and a cafe.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:04.060
Like it's pretty normal out there.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:08.780
Um, they just allow you to blow up your, your posing drivers.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:19.180
It's very strange, but, um, yeah, I, I kind of liked the, the, the sort of loop here where between each race you have these decisions to make.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:25.940
Um, if you're really good at the actual raw driving, you may not like, you could probably save a bit more.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:29.600
If not, if you're struggling a bit, you can go for one of those.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:37.460
Like you can probably still go for one of those, uh, upgrades and still make a profit, but it's all about how fast you.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:43.100
You want to make that money and kind of figuring out what your break even is.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:48.700
The missiles are also like, and I think it's good Joe to flag that you only get to use them once.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.900
It just feels like such a random choice in an otherwise like pretty grounded game.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:56.640
I really don't think they were necessary.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.440
And it's one thing to make it like a, a demolition derby kind of game.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:07.760
Um, I think we saw that, uh, you know, not the same style, but like galaxy 5,000.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.000
Um, was it galaxy 5,000 or 50,000?

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:12.740
5,000. Okay, good.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.580
Yeah. Oh, it was racing in the 51st century, but yeah, galaxy 5,000.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:22.760
Um, you know, there was, there was all sorts of like combat there in addition to the racing.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:26.720
And that kind of made sense in like a futuristic space world here.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.520
It just feels so random to have like the whole car dealership and getting the car repaired.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:36.860
And also the same guy who's fixing your tires being like, and you want missiles in the front or in the back?

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:51.680
I mean, this is kind of its own dystopia that, you know, we already have this, this car dealership slash, uh, uh, slash like body shop slash gas station vertically integrated.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:53.880
The economy can't be doing very well.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:59.860
I'd say part of like, it's mostly like, it's gotta be subsidized by some blood sport.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.500
So I think, I think it actually makes sense in the world.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:11.360
Now in the, uh, computer versions of the game, there is a slight drift to the camera as you're turning.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.120
It does not stay locked on, um, the player.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.360
And there are two ways to think about this.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:24.080
One is, wow, that's a great way to convince, uh, to convey a sense of speed in the game.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:30.360
And then the other one being, wow, that's an incredibly frustrating way to like kind of lose sight of myself for a half a second,

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.200
just to have the camera get some movement on it.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.640
And I suppose you need to see it slash play it to believe it.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:44.920
But I genuinely couldn't, um, come to a decision on my own, uh, seeing the two versions side by side.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:57.060
I think that the problem with both is that it still just feels like a very smooth racing experience just with, just with the way that the, the viewpoint of the camera was just was decided and fixed upon.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:08.240
And that again, it just feels so random to add any kind of destruction aspect to this when everything is just kind of gliding and no one's really trying to hurt you.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:13.620
Otherwise, like the other racers are a pain in the ass in the sense of getting around them.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:26.940
A lot of times it's best to just like try to beat them on a turn or something because it's usually there's not room for two, uh, down any straightaway unless you're, uh, happen to be perfectly lined up to be side by side with them ahead of time.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:30.960
Cause you're most likely just going to crash into them and you're just going to eat body damage.

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:32.560
It's not like they eat body damage.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:39.080
The only way to get rid of any particular driver is obviously shoot them with a missile that will get rid of their car.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:44.720
No problem. But for the most part, nobody else is like being antagonistic towards you.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:45.980
This isn't bumper cars.

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.220
You're the only one that can use missiles, which is a strange, uh, a, a, a strange note.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:13:04.340
Um, one other way that I found that you can try, you can try to sort of guide them into oil spills or like whatever those puddles are.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:08.740
And they usually will like stupidly drive right into them.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:17.280
Um, so if you end up finding yourself on that straightaway, that's something that you can do if you can't make the perfect, uh, pass.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:21.340
But it's very, you know, location specific, obviously.

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:23.800
Um, yeah.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:35.580
I was also trying to drift at first without looking at the manual or anything, just kind of doing like a, oh, like as I'm approaching a turn, like what if I try to break and turn at the same time?

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:37.340
And I would just like stall out or whatever.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:45.220
It, it turns out that you, you need to be like accelerating and using the brake kind of like as a handbrake while turning.

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:52.320
And it's a very like, it's a very skill based thing that I didn't get right every time.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:14:01.700
But when you do it, it is very satisfying to, you know, to hit the actual apex of these tracks so that you are making perfect turns.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.200
And it's a fun thing to get right.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:13.560
And I think this is the one aspect, uh, that this top down makes really fun is that you can kind of see and communicate ahead of time that everything is basically right angle turns.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:18.260
So it's about taking the track from the outside and then breaking inwards.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:23.400
And, uh, you know, maybe the, maybe the most confusing part, which you do learn quickly.

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:40.100
So I'm just saying from the beginning of the game is just because of this top down thing, also the way you turn in the game only using the left and right D pad is something that like took me, uh, you know, uh, not getting used to, but was something I didn't do right away.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:43.540
I like immediately I started driving like I would in other racing games.

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:47.940
And then it was like, oh, even though it's this way, I have to think about turning controls.

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:49.960
Yeah, exactly. Tank controls.

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:52.560
And then like, once you, once you get it, it's done.

00:14:52.740 --> 00:15:00.340
You can't say the same about the, the drift that does take some time to kind of, um, to, to time out and get right.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.160
And you still will crash into some walls every now and again, trying to do it.

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:06.440
Just by virtue of, of the camera.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:11.560
Um, I think this game could have used some like leading camera.

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:13.460
Like you're not always center.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:19.760
It would have helped with like, uh, just seeing what's ahead of you because it's very hard to memorize.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:25.900
These tracks. Cause you only get five laps to see it and you don't really see that far in any direction.

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:27.760
And they're all kind of similar.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:39.240
Um, and, and if they were just like, if they could just move the camera more towards the direction you're driving in, because you don't really need to see what's behind you at all.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:46.400
Like, because there's no missiles, uh, from the enemies, uh, you, you can kind of just ignore that.

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:52.860
Uh, maybe have a couple inches of, of visual space dedicated to what's behind you.

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:54.360
But like, I don't know.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:56.640
It, it, it seems like a pretty no brainer.

00:15:56.780 --> 00:16:01.340
Maybe there was some technical limitations there, but, uh, that would have helped a lot.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:04.600
The cars definitely have way too perfect traction.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:13.780
So I'll, if you're not drifting intentionally, uh, and not going over a puddle, then yes, it's going to feel like you're kind of stuck to the ground.

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:25.080
Uh, director Howard Hughes, when he was trying to film Hell's Angels, uh, he was trying to convince the, uh, the same convey the same sense of speed.

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:28.240
I keep using convey and sense together as convince.

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:29.460
Has anybody, yeah.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:30.360
Has anybody seen that?

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:31.640
That happened twice this episode.

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:46.640
Um, when he was trying to convey a, a great sense of speed on film for the planes, it was proving impossible to do on camera because there were no clouds in the sky behind them.

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:50.340
So there was nothing, there was no reference point of them passing anything.

00:16:50.340 --> 00:17:07.980
And while there's plenty of, uh, things to pass by in this, um, you know, like along the track and whatever, very rarely was I like behind or, or near like other cars in the, in this game.

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:25.160
I found it to be that, you know, at the very beginning of the race, you were chasing these cars and then you were either, um, greatly ahead of everybody or, you know, you were kind of caught in the mix up and you were in like second place and first place was already very far ahead.

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:27.160
So now you were still alone.

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:38.540
And I think that is when this game feels really slow is when there are no other cars to compete against because there's nothing else to reference in terms of, you know, motion.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:41.040
There's nothing to be like, that's what I'm chasing after.

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:47.540
Everything is just gliding with you because everything in the camera is tracking at the same speed of the, as the car.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:54.800
One thing I would also note is I, I just have a feeling that none of us played into the late game where you get the most expensive car.

00:17:55.360 --> 00:18:03.000
And I have a, I have a feeling too, that that might go a little bit faster because there is a bit of a difference between.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:06.200
Oh yeah. Even when you get the, yeah.

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:08.900
Even when you get the high speed car, right?

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:19.240
Yeah. The high speed upgrade or if you go to the next car up, there is a noticeable difference, but it's, it's not that insane, at least in terms of like the feel of the game.

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:23.680
Um, but maybe once it get up, gets up there, it starts to really whip.

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:28.080
I don't know. Yeah, maybe, but I think it would just be that it scrolls faster.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:37.660
